sql("SET LOCAL lock_timeout = '5s'")
sql("SELECT set_config('statement_timeout', COALESCE(NULLIF(current_setting('hanko.trust_migration_statement_timeout', true), ''), '30s'), true)")
sql("UPDATE trusted_devices SET tenant_id = users.tenant_id FROM users WHERE trusted_devices.user_id = users.id AND users.tenant_id IS NOT NULL AND trusted_devices.tenant_id IS NULL")
sql("DROP INDEX IF EXISTS trusted_devices_token_user_idx")
# Second step of per-clinic device trust (archon#1085 task 34). 20261101235835 added the per-tenant
# partial indexes and kept this pair index because the image before it upserts with
# ON CONFLICT (device_token, user_id), which only this index can arbitrate. With it gone, one browser
# holds the same person's trust for two clinics side by side, keyed by
# trusted_devices_token_user_tenant_idx / trusted_devices_token_user_global_idx. The current
# TrustedDevicePersister.Create names no conflict target, so it needs no code change.
#
# ROLLBACK FLOOR. Deploy this only once no pod older than 20261101235835's image is left on the
# cluster, and before multi_tenant.keep_users_global goes on there. From then on an image older than
# 20261101235835 cannot be rolled back to: its ON CONFLICT has no arbiter and every trust write
# fails. Run this migration's down first.
#
# Backfill first: a tenant-bound user's row still without a tenant (written by an older image after
# 20261101235835's own backfill) takes the user's tenant. While the pair index stands there is at
# most one row per (device_token, user_id), so this cannot collide with
# trusted_devices_token_user_tenant_idx; after the drop the same UPDATE could, once that user has
# trusted the browser again for their clinic. It touches only those leftover rows, and
# statement_timeout (hanko.trust_migration_statement_timeout, 30 s by default) bounds it.
#
# No live-row size gate here, unlike 20261101235835 and this migration's down. The UPDATE reads
# without blocking logins and row-locks only the few leftover rows (20261101235835 already
# backfilled the rest); ACCESS EXCLUSIVE comes only with the DROP INDEX, right before commit. A
# timeout rolls the whole migration back and the init container retries while the old pods serve.
# A gate would instead make every rollout of this image above 50,000 live rows a drained Hanko, a
# login outage for every clinic. The down keeps its gate: it dedupes and builds a unique index.
#
# lock_timeout: DROP INDEX takes ACCESS EXCLUSIVE on trusted_devices for an instant. Queued behind a
# long transaction it would hold every login read behind it; with the timeout it fails after 5 s
# and the init container retries while the old pods keep serving.
#
# Comments must stay BELOW the sql() calls: .fizz is run through plush.RunScript, and a leading
# comment corrupts the token stream ("no prefix parse function for ) found").
